From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 16:21:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: chrome:// pages

chrome:// URLs led nowhere. Chrome's iOS WebUI, which serves
chrome://version, chrome://flags, chrome://credits and the like, is
built on ios/web, and the Blink port connected none of it. Connect what
WebKit's CRWWebUISchemeHandler and WebStateImpl provide:

- Loading: IOSContentBrowserClient serves chrome:// navigations, and the
  subresources of chrome:// pages, from the BrowserState's network
  stack, which handles the scheme with the data sources the WebUI
  registers (URLDataManagerIOSBackend). content used its own WebUI
  loader for its WebUI schemes without asking the embedder; on iOS it
  now asks first for navigations (NavigationURLLoaderImpl::Start()) and
  lets the embedder's factory replace its own for subresources
  (RenderFrameHostImpl). The requests go without the frame's DevTools
  throttling profile: the BrowserState's network stack runs on the IO
  thread, and the throttling state may only be used on the thread of
  content's network service (the next ordinary page hit a DCHECK).
- The WebUI: ContentWebState creates the page's WebUI controller when a
  navigation to it starts, as WebStateImpl does, and drops it when the
  tab goes elsewhere. The controller's MojoFacade expects an interface
  binder for the main frame, which ContentWebState now has; it was null,
  and the first page crashed the app as it finished loading.
- Committing: content only lets processes with its own WebUI bindings
  commit chrome:// pages, and ends any other renderer that does: here
  the in-process one, and the app with it. ContentWebState grants the
  renderer the chrome scheme before the commit.
- chrome.send(): web_ui.js posts to the "WebUIMessage" handler.
  WebUIMessagingJavaScriptFeature handles it for WebKit but casts the
  WebState to WebStateImpl, so ContentWebFramesManager gives these
  messages to ContentWebState, which checks them the same way and hands
  them to the WebUI.

Pages whose WebUI talks Mojo still show nothing (chrome://chrome-urls
only its title): MojoFacade needs ExecuteAsyncJavaScript(), which
ContentWebFrame doesn't implement yet (crbug.com/507056709).

Status: verified at Chromium 3313f827 in the iOS 27 simulator:
chrome://version shows the version, OS, user agent and command line
with the Chromium logo; chrome://flags lists the experiments (asked for
with chrome.send() and returned by the handler); chrome://credits shows
the licenses (with generate_about_credits = true in the build's args,
the sample page otherwise); Wikipedia, GitHub and bilibili load as
before, also right after a chrome:// page; chrome://chrome-urls shows
only its title. Not checked on a device.

